WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.430
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.787 --> 00:00:07.870
ROGER MARTINEZ: Welcome
to the Quickstart tutorial

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:09.460
for Cloud Endpoints.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:11.950
Let's get started using
Cloud Endpoints by activating

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:13.345
the Cloud Shell in our console.

00:00:18.405 --> 00:00:20.405
We'll download the project
code to our instance.

00:00:26.458 --> 00:00:28.250
If we take a look at
the scripts directory,

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:30.500
we'll see that we've got a
couple of scripts in there.

00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:35.990
Let's start by
running deploy_api.sh.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:42.570
This script will deploy an API
included in the project files

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:46.140
to Cloud Endpoints.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.750
Next, let's deploy
the API backend,

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:53.230
by executing deploy_app.sh.

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:55.455
This will create an App
Engine flexible environment

00:00:55.455 --> 00:00:58.510
to host the API Backend
and deploy our sample

00:00:58.510 --> 00:00:59.710
API to App Engine.

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:09.350
To see our API in action,
we'll run query_api.sh.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:11.690
This command takes
one argument--

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:13.710
a three-letter airport code.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:18.120
For example, if we
enter EWR, our API

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:22.810
returns Newark Liberty
International Airport.

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:23.530
Cool.

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:26.470
We've just deployed and
tested an API in Endpoints.

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:28.450
Once we have an API
deployed, we can also

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:30.700
track API activity
and gain insight

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:34.270
into our users and usage
with Stackdriver logging.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:36.100
In order to do
that, first, let's

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.500
enable the Service Control
API on our project.

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:51.450
Now, let's generate some
activity to work with.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:54.900
The generate_traffic.sh
script will generate requests

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:56.970
to our API for five minutes.

00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:00.850
That will give us plenty of
data to check out metrics for.

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:02.560
Once traffic has
been generated, we

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.540
can check out the
Endpoints Services page.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.210
Right off the bat,
we've got visibility

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:11.070
into the number of
requests per second,

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:12.795
500 errors, and latency.

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:18.190
We can check out the logs
for the methods of our API

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:20.040
through this link at
the bottom of the page.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.520
We just deployed our API
and started generating data,

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.780
so there aren't that
many errors to be seen.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.260
But as you can see,
every request to our API

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:32.180
is logged with
details, like timestamp

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:35.600
of the request, the method
called, and the HTTP response

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:38.140
code.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:39.400
And that's it.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:41.890
That's how easy it is to get
set up with Cloud Endpoints

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:43.750
to deploy your very own API.

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:46.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

